{"id":"2010-06-17-wadena","name":"Wadena","region":"Minnesota","blurb":"A wide EF4 through Wadena on Minnesota's biggest tornado day on record.","scale":"EF","tz":"America/Chicago","T0":1276807380,"source":"NOAA Storm Prediction Center tornado database (1950\u20132025)","pathFidelity":"surveyed centrelines from NOAA's Damage Assessment Toolkit","torn":[{"name":"Douglas\u2013Otter Tail, MN","ef":"EF4","start":0,"end":3720,"coords":[[-95.34182,46.19854],[-95.34012,46.19985],[-95.33937,46.20065],[-95.3383,46.20178],[-95.33761,46.20277]],"lengthMi":39.56,"widthYd":10.0,"inj":5,"fat":1,"state":"MN","geometry":"surveyed","timing":"surveyed","comments":"Satellite imagery shows two distinct scars presumed to have been caused by satellite tornados. This is the western scar of the two. As no damage to anything except cropland is visible a rating is not possible and thus both are being assigned EFU ratings. Radar suggests both presumed satellite tornados occurred around 4pm on the day tracking under a half mile each and lasting less than 1 minute given a roughly 40-45kt storm motion of the parent storm.","wfo":"FGF"},{"name":"Otter Tail, MN","ef":"EF1","start":3900,"end":4020,"coords":[[-95.22288,46.31573],[-95.22554,46.31454],[-95.22838,46.31266],[-95.23098,46.31094],[-95.2349,46.30909],[-95.23889,46.30725],[-95.24086,46.30559],[-95.24275,46.30331],[-95.24503,46.30109],[-95.24756,46.29926],[-95.2515,46.29678],[-95.25329,46.29604],[-95.2572,46.29355],[-95.26368,46.28817],[-95.26587,46.28622],[-95.26861,46.28484],[-95.27253,46.28243],[-95.27398,46.28132],[-95.27574,46.28029],[-95.27722,46.27939],[-95.27904,46.27793]],"lengthMi":1.52,"widthYd":115.0,"inj":0,"fat":0,"state":"MN","geometry":"surveyed","timing":"surveyed","maxwind":100,"comments":"This tornado tracked briefly to the north-northeast before a wet rear-flank downdraft overtook it as viewed and recorded by multiple storm chase teams. A second and more powerful tornado eventually formed from the parent supercell thunderstorm and impacted the the community of Wadena. Very heavy rains and damaging downdraft winds obscured the overall tornado path. However large tree limbs were broken down and smaller trees were uprooted by the event suggesting peak winds to 90 mph.\n\nSatellite analysis reveals that mutilple farm buildings were also impacted by this tornado ranging from major loss of roofing to complete destruction of at least one building. The quality of the buildings strength area unknown and the rating trended towards the lower bound of the exp DI at 100 mph","wfo":"FGF"},{"name":"Otter Tail\u2013Wadena, MN","ef":"EF4","start":4560,"end":5580,"coords":[[-95.16894,46.39861],[-95.16276,46.40464],[-95.15804,46.41299],[-95.15314,46.41855],[-95.15125,46.42713],[-95.14825,46.44008],[-95.14456,46.44689],[-95.13332,46.45623],[-95.12576,46.46084],[-95.11546,46.47042],[-95.11272,46.47698],[-95.10499,46.48106],[-95.09401,46.49087],[-95.08431,46.49625],[-95.07727,46.50103],[-95.06663,46.50605]],"lengthMi":10.0,"widthYd":700.0,"inj":20,"fat":0,"state":"MN","geometry":"surveyed","timing":"surveyed","maxwind":170,"comments":"This tornado touched down in Otter Tail County and tracked northeastward for about 2 miles before crossing into Wadena County just southwest of the Wadena city limits. The tornado developed rapidly into a multi-vortex wedge type tornado as it approached Wadena. The tornado continued in Wadena County another 8 miles and ended about 511 PM CDT. Otter Tail County received mainly extreme tree damage with peak winds estimated at 170 mph. \n\nThis multi-vortex wedge tornado began in Otter Tail County about 3 miles southwest of the city of Wadena at 452 PM CDT. It tracked for about 2 miles in Otter Tail County, then it continued for another 8 miles in Wadena County. The tornado ravaged the western half of the community of Wadena. Numerous homes were flattened to the ground and at least one was completely swept from its foundation and destroyed. Public and industrial buildings were wrecked, the fairgrounds were damaged, and headstones in the cemetery were overturned. School buses and vehicles were propelled through the air for hundreds of yards. The tornado hit on the day of the Wadena all-school reunion, so many additional people were in the area. Roughly 100 people were displaced from their homes. Peak winds were estimated at 170 mph.","wfo":"FGF"},{"name":"Wadena\u2013Cass, MN","ef":"EF0","start":6420,"end":7500,"coords":[[-94.85929,46.62185],[-94.6684,46.78854]],"lengthMi":14.76,"widthYd":200.0,"inj":0,"fat":0,"state":"MN","geometry":"surveyed","timing":"surveyed","maxwind":80,"comments":"The tornado tracked east-northeastward intermittently for about 5 miles, where it reached the Cass County line east of Nimrod. The tornado continued northeast into Cass County for several more miles. Numerous large tree branches were broken down along this path. Peak winds were estimated at 80 mph.\n\nThe tornado continued from Wadena County. Trees were snapped along its path, including County Road 2 and 64 up to the west side of Deer Lake.","wfo":"DLH,FGF"}],"anchor":"Otter Tail\u2013Wadena, MN","dpts":[[46.1671,-95.3587,"EF2",115,"Small Barns or Farm Outbuildings (SBO)","Total destruction of building",0],[46.1949,-95.3616,"EF1",100,"Small Barns or Farm Outbuildings (SBO)","Total destruction of building",0],[46.1982,-95.3551,"EF0",0,"Other (O)","Other damage",0],[46.2903,-95.2621,"EF1",100,"Small Barns or Farm Outbuildings (SBO)","Collapse of walls",1],[46.3083,-95.2367,"EF1",100,"Small Barns or Farm Outbuildings (SBO)","Total destruction of building",1],[46.3075,-95.2366,"EF1",90,"Small Barns or Farm Outbuildings (SBO)","Major loss of roof panels",1]],"hail":[[-9960.0,46.82,-94.51,1.75,"BACKUS, MN","NWS EMPLOYEE","TIME IS APPROXIMATE. 1 INCH DIAMETER HAIL WAS OBSERVED STILL LAYING ON THE GROUND AT 135 PM...AND WAS LIKELY LARGER WHEN IT FELL. OTHER OBSERVERS SAID THERE WAS SOME GOLFBALL SIZE HAIL WHEN IT ORIGINALLY FELL."],[-9960.0,46.79,-94.51,1.75,"2 